import pf.Html
import ../../Ui/Heading
import ../../Ui/Callout
import ../../Ui/Code
import ../../Generated/Sources
import ../../Generated/Paths
import ../../Ui/PageHero
import ../../Ui/TableWrap

meta = {
	title: "Tooling",
	description: "rocx dev, build, check, lint, routes and fmt; compiler errors reported at your .rocx lines; highlighting in an editor; setting up an app.",
	toc: [
		{ id: "commands", label: "Commands" },
		{ id: "errors", label: "Errors at your lines" },
		{ id: "editors", label: "In an editor" },
		{ id: "stories", label: "Stories" },
		{ id: "setup", label: "Setting up an app" },
		{ id: "repository", label: "This repository" },
	],
}

view! = |_|
	<>
		<NoHydration>
			<PageHero
				eyebrow="Tooling"
				title="A few commands, and the compiler you already have"
			>
				rocx is a transpiler, written in Roc and built into one executable (<code>bin/rocx</code>): it
				turns markup into Roc, and the Roc compiler does the rest. The tooling is about keeping that
				invisible.
			</PageHero>

			<Heading id="commands">Commands</Heading>
			<div class="commands">
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx dev {"[dir]"}<span>--port 8080</span></p>
					<p>
						Builds the app in the directory (or here) and serves it at
						<code>http://localhost:8080</code> under <code>--dev</code>; each save builds it again and
						restarts the server, and its open pages reload. A mistake is reported at its
						<code>.rocx</code> line while the build before is still served. An app without a
						<code>main.roc</code> gets the smallest one. What follows <code>--</code> goes to the server
						(<code>--database-url</code>), and so does the environment (<code>DATABASE_URL</code>).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx build {"<dir>…"}<span>--client {"<out>"}</span></p>
					<div>
						<p>
							Each <code>.rocx</code> under the directories becomes the <code>.roc</code> beside it, its
							markup written with the platform's <code>Html</code> (<code>import pf.Html</code>), with
							computations and handlers. An app's <code>routes/</code> become
							<code>Generated/Routes.roc</code>, <code>Paths.roc</code>, with endpoints, <code>Api.roc</code>,
							and <code>Generated/App.roc</code>, the program its
							<code>main.roc</code> gives what only it can say (<code>App.program</code>, with the jobs the app
							defines and its <code>migrations/*.sql</code>). An app with pages (<code>view!</code> in a
							route file) also gets <code>Generated/Root.roc</code>, the page at the browser's path, in its
							layouts. <code>--client</code> also writes the browser's copy of the app, where an endpoint in
							<code>Api</code> is only its method and URL, a route file has no <code>middleware</code>, a
							<code>{"<NoHydration>"}</code> has no children, and the imports only those used are gone; for
							an app with pages, its <code>main.roc</code> and <code>static/rocx/</code>, where start.js
							loads the runtime and wasm, beside the app's stylesheet, named by its content
							(<code>styles-<var>hash</var>.css</code>). The app's own <code>main.roc</code>, the server's program, stays out
							of the copy, so an app of endpoints alone has no browser's program, and the build says so.
							If the browser's app reaches a module that imports what only the server has
							(<code>pf.Db</code>, itself or through another module), the build warns at the import a page or
							component makes, before the browser's build fails far from it.
						</p>
					</div>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx check -- {"<command>"}</p>
					<p>
						Runs a command (<code>roc check</code>, <code>roc build</code>) with its reports pointed into
						the <code>.rocx</code> files: <a href="#errors">errors at your lines</a>.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx check --sql {"[dir]"}<span>--engine turso</span><span>--verbose</span></p>
					<p>
						Checks the app's SQL with its own database engine, and its rows with the compiler: each statement
						written as a string at a <code>Db.all!</code> or <code>Db.run!</code>, prepared on a scratch
						database built from the app's migrations. What the engine refuses, a call whose parameters don't
						match its statement, and a row record that doesn't fit its columns are reported at their lines
						(<a href={"${Paths.platform_database}#checked"}>how</a>). <code>rocx dev</code> runs it after each
						build.
						<code>--engine turso</code> asks Turso instead of SQLite; <code>--verbose</code> lists what it
						couldn't check.
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx lint {"<dir>…"}</p>
					<p>
						Refuses <code>Html.source_text</code> in hand-written <code>.roc</code> files, where it would
						put any string in a page as markup (<a href={"${Paths.safety}#lint"}>why</a>).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx routes {"<dir>"}</p>
					<p>
						Lists an app's routes: each method a path answers, the file that answers it, and the
						middleware that file runs behind, after the app's default
						(<a href={"${Paths.routing}#middleware"}>what runs, in what order</a>).
					</p>
				</div>
				<div class="command">
					<p class="command-usage">rocx fmt {"<path>…"}<span>--check</span></p>
					<p>
						Formats the Roc in each <code>.rocx</code> file, and each <code>.roc</code> file rocx didn't
						write, with <code>roc fmt</code>. Markup, and the Roc in its holes, stay as written: a piece
						of markup moves only with the code around it, and a <code>{"<pre>"}</code>'s text never moves.
						<code>--check</code> only says which files aren't formatted.
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p>
				<code>build</code> writes a file only when its content changes, so a watcher that sees it doesn't
				build forever; deletes the <code>.roc</code> files it wrote whose <code>.rocx</code> is gone; and stops
				at the first markup problem with its line and column. Generated files end with a
				<code># rocx: generated from …</code> line, and stay out of version control.
			</p>

			<Heading id="errors">Errors at your lines</Heading>
			<p>
				Because line <var>N</var> of the generated file is line <var>N</var> of yours, the compiler's reports
				already name the right line. <code>rocx check</code> makes them read as if the compiler had seen
				your file: it rewrites the location, quotes your line instead of the generated one, and moves the
				caret onto the same code; a mistake in a route file's Roc (<code>+server.roc</code>) is put at its
				line too. Here is a mistake, a number where text belongs:
			</p>
			<Code title="Posts.rocx" source={Sources.check_source} />
			<p>and what <code>rocx check -- roc check Posts.roc</code> printed when this page was built:</p>
			<div class="report"><Code title="The compiler, through rocx check" source={Sources.check_report} numbers={Bool.False} plain /></div>
			<Callout kind="tip" title="The fix">
				<p>
					<code>{"{posts.len().to_str()}"}</code>: a text hole takes a <code>Str</code>, and Roc never turns
					a number into one by itself.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<Heading id="editors">In an editor</Heading>
			<p>
				The repository's <code>editors/orca</code> is a plugin for <a href="https://github.com/stablyai/orca">Orca</a>
				that highlights <code>.rocx</code> files and <code>.roc</code> files: Roc by the official Roc grammar,
				markup (an element's tag, a component's as a type, attributes like <code>class:active</code> and
				<code>on:click.prevent</code>), the Roc in holes, markup in that Roc, and CSS in a
				<code>{"<style>"}</code>. It runs nothing, so it installs without asking for access:
				<code>orca plugin install editors/orca</code>. Its grammars are TextMate grammars, which VS Code and
				most editors read too. The one guess it makes is whether a <code>{"<"}</code> starts markup: before a
				letter, unless a value is right before it, so <code>{"a <b"}</code> (a space before and none after)
				reads as markup. Errors stay with <code>rocx check</code>; there is no language server yet.
			</p>

			<Heading id="stories">Stories</Heading>
			<p>
				A component's stories are the ways it looks, written beside it: a <code>{"<story name=\"…\">"}</code>
				in its block, among its definitions, holds the markup of one. Each becomes a definition in its
				place (<code>story_tip</code>), so every build checks it against the component, and only a dev
				build calls it. With <code>just dev</code> (<code>rocx build --dev</code>), the server has a
				catalog of them at <code>/__rocx/stories</code>: every story down the side, which folds away
				(<kbd>S</kbd>); the one picked in a frame, with the line it is written at, which can fill the
				window (<kbd>F</kbd>, and <kbd>Esc</kbd> to come back); and a component's stories side by side.
				A story is the app's page at its path, which <code>Root</code> renders in the root layout and the
				browser hydrates; the layout can tell it is a story by its path (<code>/__rocx/stories/…</code>):
				this site's shows it on its own. The site's own build, and what <code>just site</code> publishes,
				has no stories at all.
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.ui_callout.file} source={Sources.ui_callout.rocx} />
			<p>
				An app's <code>stories/</code> directory holds modules of stories alone: drafts of something new, each a
				component with its own <code>{"<style>"}</code>, side by side in the catalog. Only a dev build
				has it, so nothing in it is published, its styles neither. This site's
				<code>site/stories/Landing.rocx</code> has the front page as it is, and drafts of it in six
				themes.
			</p>

			<Heading id="setup">Setting up an app</Heading>
			<p>
				<code>rocx dev</code> does what follows for you as you edit
				(<a href={"${Paths.root}#start"}>getting started</a>). By hand, or for a build that ships: run
				<code>rocx build</code> before <code>roc build</code>, and <code>rocx check</code> instead of
				<code>roc check</code>. An app is two programs (<a href={Paths.platform_}>the platform</a>): the
				server's, on the server platform (native, in a Rust host), and the browser's, the same pages, on the
				web platform (wasm). With pages in <code>routes/</code> (<a href={Paths.routing}>routing</a>), the
				server's app answers every request through the router rocx writes (the GETs a page reads as it
				renders too), behind its default middleware. rocx writes that program
				(<code>Generated/App.roc</code>); the app's <code>main.roc</code> says what only it can:
			</p>
			<Code
				title="main.roc"
				source={"app [program] { pf: platform \"../rocx/server/platform/main.roc\" }\n\nimport pf.Middleware\nimport Generated/App\n\nprogram = App.program({\n\tinit!: |_started| Ok({}),\n\tmiddleware: Middleware.same_origin({}),\n})"}
			/>
			<p>
				The browser's app is rocx's to write (<code>rocx build --client</code>): its root is the page,
				which the browser hydrates. This site's:
			</p>
			<Code title={Sources.client_app.file} source={Sources.client_app.rocx} />
			<Code
				title="Justfile"
				source={"build:\n\t# The browser platform's host, a Zig object the wasm links with (once; site/build.mjs does the same)\n\tzig build-obj rocx/web/host/host.zig -target wasm32-freestanding -O ReleaseSmall -fcompiler-rt -femit-bin=rocx/web/platform/targets/wasm32/host.wasm\n\trocx/bin/rocx build app --client build/client\n\troc build --target=wasm32 --opt=size --specialize=yes --output=build/client/static/rocx/app.wasm build/client/main.roc\n\tnode rocx/server/build.mjs app/main.roc build/server --opt=speed\n\nserve:\n\tbuild/server --port 8080 --static build/client/static\n\ncheck:\n\trocx/bin/rocx lint app\n\trocx/bin/rocx check -- roc check app/main.roc"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
				plain
			/>
			<p>
				The two halves are optimized differently. The server's, <code>--opt=speed</code>, is built
				with boxy lowering (<code>--specialize=no</code>): specialized, Roc inlines every closure of
				a lambda set where it is called, and an app's pages all sit behind one such call, so that
				function's stack frame grows with the app. This site's reached 12.4 MB, past the 8 MiB a
				render runs on. Boxy keeps every app's largest frame under 32 KB, renders as fast and builds
				in a fraction of the time. <code>--specialize=yes</code> (or <code>ROCX_SPECIALIZE=yes</code>)
				asks for specialization back. The browser's wasm stays specialized: it is smaller with
				brotli and renders faster (DECISIONS.md, D-064, has the numbers).
			</p>
			<p>
				Requests, routes, endpoints, events and middleware are both platforms' (<code>pf.Server</code>,
				<code>pf.Endpoint</code>, <code>pf.Middleware</code>, <code>pf.Events</code>, <code>pf.OpenApi</code>),
				and so are the modules components are written with, so the same component compiles on both.
				Pages need the <code>web/</code> platform in the browser, and the server platform to render them,
				natively; the database (Postgres or SQLite), channels, live rooms and actors are the server platform's, and jobs, cron
				and email are packages for it (<a href={Paths.ecosystem}>the ecosystem</a>).
			</p>

			<Heading id="repository">This repository</Heading>
			<TableWrap>
				<table>
					<thead><tr><th>Where</th><th>What</th></tr></thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr><td><code>rocx-cli/</code></td><td>rocx: the transpiler and the command, <code>rocx dev</code> and <code>just dev</code>'s loop included, in Roc, built into <code>bin/rocx</code></td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>cli/</code></td><td>the command-line platform rocx is built on: files, commands, a directory tree watched, signals, and one wait for whichever comes first; a Rust host</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>web/</code></td><td>the browser's platform: its Roc header, the modules both platforms have (components'; and requests, endpoints, events, middleware), the Zig host, the runtime</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>server/</code></td><td>the server's platform: its Roc header and modules, the Rust host, the build</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>site/</code></td><td>this site: its pages in <code>routes/</code>, in rocx; <code>rocx site dev</code> (<code>just dev</code>) builds and serves them as you edit, <code>build.mjs</code> builds the static site</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>spike/</code></td><td>experiments: <code>spike/suspense</code>, how a read that has to wait can stop a computation in wasm</td></tr>
						<tr><td><code>test/</code>, <code>web/test/</code>, <code>site/test/</code>, <code>server/test/</code></td><td>the transpiler, the browser's platform, the site in a real browser, the server's platform</td></tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</TableWrap>
			<p>
				Zig 0.17.1 and Rust ≥1.85 from your system (e.g. Nix), or <code>just setup</code> downloads them.
				Roc is a fork of the pinned nightly with the compiler fixes rocx needs, which <code>just setup</code>
				builds into <code>.tools/</code>.
			</p>
			<Code
				source={"just setup   # the Roc fork rocx builds with, the pinned nightly, Zig and Rust if need be, into .tools/; then bin/rocx\njust db      # a Postgres in Docker, for the server platform\njust test    # everything: the transpiler, both platforms, the site in Chromium\njust dev     # this site, rendered per request, rebuilt and reloaded on save\njust site    # the static site, in site/dist"}
				numbers={Bool.False}
			/>
		</NoHydration>
	</>

<style>
	/* The commands, a card each: how it is written, and what it does. */
	.commands {
		display: grid;
		gap: var(--space-3);
		margin: var(--space-4) 0;
	}

	.command {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
		gap: var(--space-4);
		padding: var(--space-4) var(--space-5);
		border-radius: var(--radius);
		background: var(--bg-raised);
	}

	.command-usage {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--accent);
		font: 600 var(--size-sm) / 1.6 var(--mono);
	}

	.command-usage span {
		display: block;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-weight: 400;
	}

	.command p:not(.command-usage) {
		margin: 0;
		color: var(--text-muted);
		font-size: var(--size-sm);
	}

	.command p:not(.command-usage) + p {
		margin-top: var(--space-2);
	}

	/* The compiler's report, as it prints it: its lines aren't wrapped. */
	.report :global(pre) {
		white-space: pre;
	}

	@media (max-width: 52rem) {
		.command {
			grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
		}
	}
</style>
